--------------------------------------------------------------------------
ATIF (Agent Trajectory Interchange Format) models
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Everything under `core/trajectory/` is a derived work of Harbor's ATIF model
package, `src/harbor/models/trajectories/`:

    __init__.py                 agent.py
    content.py                  final_metrics.py
    metrics.py                  observation.py
    observation_result.py       step.py
    subagent_trajectory_ref.py  tool_call.py
    trajectory.py

The port is faithful. Import paths were rewritten for this package, attribution
headers were added to each module's docstring, and `trajectory.py` gained a
`SCHEMA_VERSION` constant naming the ATIF version this repository emits. No
field was removed, renamed or retyped: an interchange format that dropped the
fields one consumer did not need would no longer be interchangeable.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Codex rollout -> ATIF conversion
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`CodexAgent.to_trajectory` and its helpers in `core/agents/codex.py` are ported
from
Harbor's `src/harbor/agents/installed/codex.py`, specifically the methods
`_extract_message_text`, `_parse_output_blob`, `_group_events_by_api_call_id`,
`_metrics_from_token_count_payload`, `_convert_event_to_step` and
`_convert_events_to_trajectory`.

Documented divergences, each explained in a comment at the site:

  * Harbor reads the rollout `.jsonl` from disk after copying the Codex session
    directory out of the environment. This sandbox has no bind mount, so the
    events arrive in memory from the in-container runner and the file-discovery
    half of Harbor's method is absent. Event handling is unchanged.
  * Harbor prices each API call through LiteLLM on the host. The container has
    no pricing table, so per-call `cost_usd` is left null rather than estimated;
    the token counts, which the rollout does carry, are recorded exactly.
  * Harbor discards any step whose conversion raises `ValueError`. Because
    pydantic's `ValidationError` subclasses `ValueError`, a rollout with
    unparseable timestamps loses EVERY step that way. This port tolerates a
    non-ISO-8601 timestamp (dropping the timestamp, keeping the step) and
    records any step it still could not convert in the trajectory's `notes`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Claude Agent SDK -> ATIF conversion
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`ClaudeSDKAgent.to_trajectory` follows the ATIF semantics of Harbor's
`src/harbor/agents/installed/claude_code.py` `_convert_events_to_trajectory`,
and `ClaudeSDKAgent._metrics` is a port of that file's `_build_metrics`
(including its guard against null usage fields on an interrupted stream). The
surrounding walk is a hand mapping rather than a port: Harbor parses Claude
Code's on-disk JSONL session transcript, whereas this repository drives the
Claude Agent SDK, whose message objects are a different shape.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Agent failure taxonomy
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`core/agents/errors.py` is a port of the error taxonomy in Harbor's
`src/harbor/agents/installed/base.py`: the `ApiError` hierarchy (rate limit,
usage limit, internal server, overloaded, connection closed, response stalled,
output token exceeded, context window exceeded, safety refusal, unknown), the
`AgentAuthenticationError` / `ModelNotFoundError` / `NetworkConnectionError`
classes beside it, the `ErrorPattern` mechanism and Harbor's pattern list with
its ordering and its "last match wins" and soft-refusal rules.

Documented divergences:

  * Harbor's root class is `NonZeroAgentExitCodeError`, named for the mechanism
    that raises it: a command Harbor exec'd into a live environment returned
    non-zero. This sandbox has no exec channel -- an episode is one
    `enroot start` that returns one JSON record -- so the root class is
    `AgentError`, the same taxonomy without the mechanism in the name.
  * Harbor RAISES these and its retry policy catches them. This repository
    CLASSIFIES with them: the class is a label on an episode record, so that a
    context-window exhaustion and a provider safety refusal are separable in
    analysis instead of collapsing into one `error` string.
  * Harbor hangs the patterns off `BaseInstalledAgent`. Ours are on the `Agent`
    contract, because the text being classified is the record every adapter
    returns rather than a command this process ran.
  * The `HarnessError` subtree (container launch, setup hook, install, timeout,
    container exit, no record, unparseable record) has no Harbor equivalent and
    is not derived from Harbor.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Agent adapter structure
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`core/agents/` follows the layout and the separation of concerns of Harbor's
`src/harbor/agents/`: the contract in `base.py` (`BaseAgent`), the machinery for
an agent that must be installed into the environment in `installed.py`
(`BaseInstalledAgent`), one module per adapter as in `installed/*.py`, and a
name -> "module:Class" registry imported on demand in `factory.py`
(`AgentFactory._AGENT_MAP`).

One deliberate divergence is documented at `InstalledAgent.install()`: Harbor's
`BaseInstalledAgent.install()` EXECUTES against a live environment, while ours
RETURNS a check/install command pair, because an episode here is a single
`enroot start` with no exec channel.

Node runtime provisioning (nvm version, install shape) also follows Harbor's
approach for its Node-based agents.
